em_crypto: write the final decrypted block into the caller's buffer.

platform_cipher_decrypt() throws away the last block of every decryption and
reports it to the caller as valid plaintext.

    uint8_t buf[AES_BLOCK_SIZE];              /* local scratch */
    ...
    plen = data_len;
    EVP_DecryptUpdate(ctx, data, &plen, data, data_len);   /* in-place */

    len = sizeof(buf);
    EVP_DecryptFinal_ex(ctx, buf, &len);      /* final block -> local, discarded */
    plen += len;                              /* ...but still counted */

For AES-128-CBC the callers pass disable_padding = false, so PKCS#7 padding is
enabled and EVP_DecryptUpdate deliberately holds the last block back until
EVP_DecryptFinal_ex can strip the padding. That final block's plaintext is
written into the local `buf`, which is never read and goes out of scope. The
decryption is in-place, so the corresponding bytes of the caller's buffer are
never written and still contain the raw CIPHERTEXT -- while `plen` is
incremented by their length, telling the caller they are valid plaintext.

Result: every decryption silently returns correct plaintext up to the last
block boundary, followed by ciphertext bytes presented as plaintext.

Found via the EasyMesh WSC M2 encrypted settings, where it corrupted the
fronthaul radio identity. Dumping the plaintext at both ends and diffing:

    haul 0: tx 95B  rx 95B   differing byte offsets: 80..94
    haul 1: tx 95B  rx 95B   differing byte offsets: 80..94
    haul 2: tx 90B  rx 90B   differing byte offsets: 80..89

Byte 80 is exactly 5 x AES_BLOCK_SIZE. Everything before the last block
boundary is correct in every case; everything after it is ciphertext.

Why that produced the symptom it did: in the M2 encrypted settings the WSC MAC
Address attribute is the LAST attribute written, and its value spans bytes
77-82 -- straddling the 80-byte boundary. Bytes 77-79 survive, 80-82 do not, so
the agent decoded MACs whose first three bytes were right and last three were
ciphertext. Every one of them therefore began 02:00:00 (the hwsim OUI, from the
surviving half) and matched no interface. They looked like plausible but
unknown MACs rather than garbage, which is exactly what made this hard to
place.

It also explains the apparent "positional" pattern -- hauls 0 and 1 wrong,
haul 2 right. That was never positional. It is a LENGTH effect: iot_ssid is
shorter than private_ssid and mesh_backhaul, so in that blob every attribute
sits earlier and the MAC value lands at 72-77, entirely inside the last good
block. Same code, same path, different SSID length.

And it explains why nothing else looked wrong: SSID, auth type, encryption type
and network key all sit before byte 80. The MAC was simply the only field far
enough into the buffer to be hit -- not the only field at risk.

Fix: pass `data + plen` to EVP_DecryptFinal_ex so the final block lands where it
belongs. Safe unconditionally -- plaintext is never longer than the ciphertext
the caller supplied, so the buffer always has room.

Verified on a controller and its colocated agent. Before, no received plaintext
matched what was transmitted and the decoded radio MACs matched no interface.
After, all 14 received plaintexts match a transmitted plaintext byte for byte,
and every decoded MAC is a real radio:

    ##mac address[0..2]: 02:00:00:c9:61:4e    = wifi0
    ##mac address[0..2]: 02:00:00:a3:c9:eb    = wifi2
    ##mac address[0..3]: 02:00:00:41:21:c4    = wifi1

Not gated behind HWSIM_RADIO, and not specific to EasyMesh: this is a generic
defect in a shared crypto helper, on every platform, for every caller that
decrypts with padding enabled. WSC M2 is simply where it happened to be
noticed.

Upstream: unified-wifi-mesh v0.3.1 (SRCREV c0e72a3).

---
diff --git a/src/em/crypto/em_crypto.cpp b/src/em/crypto/em_crypto.cpp
index b0a14d08..0709cd39 100644
--- a/src/em/crypto/em_crypto.cpp
+++ b/src/em/crypto/em_crypto.cpp
@@ -576,8 +576,14 @@ uint32_t em_crypto_t::platform_cipher_decrypt(const EVP_CIPHER *cipher_type, uin
         return 0;
     }
 
-    len = sizeof(buf);
-    if (EVP_DecryptFinal_ex(ctx, buf, &len) != 1) {
+    // The final block's plaintext must land in the caller's buffer, immediately
+    // after what DecryptUpdate produced. Writing it to a local scratch buffer
+    // discards it: with padding enabled DecryptUpdate holds the last block back,
+    // so data[plen..] would keep the raw ciphertext while plen below still counts
+    // those bytes as valid plaintext. Safe because the plaintext is never longer
+    // than the ciphertext the caller supplied.
+    len = static_cast<int> (sizeof(buf));
+    if (EVP_DecryptFinal_ex(ctx, data + plen, &len) != 1) {
         unsigned long err = ERR_get_error();
         char err_buf[256];
         ERR_error_string_n(err, err_buf, sizeof(err_buf));
